.TH V32C++\-VECTOR 3v32 "October 11, 2026" "v32c++ 20261011-dev" "v32c++ Library"
.SH NAME
v32c++\-vector \- std::vector in v32c++, a growable container
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <vector>
.PP
.BI "std::vector< " T " > " name ;
.PP
.BI "void push_back( const T& " value " );"
.B  "void pop_back();"
.B  "int  size();"
.B  "int  capacity();"
.B  "bool empty();"
.BI "T&   operator[]( int " i " );"
.BI "T&   at( int " i " );"
.B  "T&   front();"
.B  "T&   back();"
.B  "T*   data();"
.B  "T*   begin();"
.B  "T*   end();"
.BI "void reserve( int " n " );"
.B  "void clear();"
.BI "void resize( int " n " );"
.BI "void resize( int " n ", const T& " value " );"
.BI "T*   erase( T* " pos " );"
.BI "T*   insert( T* " pos ", const T& " value " );"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Like
.B std::array
(see
.BR v32c++\-array (3v32)),
.B std::vector
is a built-in generic type: for each element type a program names, the
transpiler writes one ordinary class,
.BI vector_ T
in the generated C
.RB ( std::vector<int>
is
.BR vector_int ).
.B #include <vector>
switches the feature on; no file is read. The element types allowed
are those of
.BR std::array .
.PP
The storage comes from the SDK's
.B malloc
and
.B free
(see
.BR misc.h (3v32)).
.SS Members
.TP
.BR push_back ", " pop_back
Add an element at the end, or remove the last. The capacity starts at
4 and doubles when full.
.TP
.BR size ", " capacity ", " empty
The number of elements, the room allocated, and whether there are no
elements.
.TP
.BR operator[] ", " at ", " front ", " back
References to elements; no bounds checking.
.TP
.BR data ", " begin ", " end
Plain
.B T*
pointers.
.TP
.BR reserve ", " clear
Make room for
.I n
elements; remove all elements, keeping the storage.
.TP
.B resize
Change the number of elements; new elements are zero-filled, or copies
of
.IR value .
.TP
.BR erase ", " insert
Remove the element at
.IR pos ,
or insert
.I value
before it.
.I pos
is a pointer:
.BR "v.begin() + i" .
.PP
.B "a = b"
copies the elements. Range-based
.B for
works over a vector, and accessors are written out in place in the
generated C unless
.B \-\-no\-inline\-containers
is given to
.BR v32c++ (1).
.SS Differences from C++
.IP \(bu 2
Elements are raw memory: they are copied in with plain assignment, and
the vector never runs an element's constructor or destructor. Numbers,
pointers, plain structs and classes whose constructor only sets fields
behave as expected, virtual functions included. A class with a
destructor gets a warning; hold pointers to such objects instead.
.IP \(bu 2
Copies other than
.B "a = b"
are errors:
.BR "std::vector<T> b = a;" ,
passing a vector by value and returning one by value would leave two
vectors owning one buffer. Pass
.BR "std::vector<T> &" .
.IP \(bu 2
Running out of memory is not reported. Call
.B reserve
when the size is known; growing copies every element.
.IP \(bu 2
A reference or pointer into a vector is invalid once the vector grows,
as in C++.
.IP \(bu 2
A vector of vectors needs a space between the closing brackets
.RB ( "std::vector<std::vector<int> >" ),
and its inner vectors are raw memory like any other element: they are
not constructed.
.IP \(bu 2
A file-scope vector is constructed at the top of
.BR main .
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
#include <vector>

struct Bullet { int x; int y; int dy; };

std::vector< Bullet > bullets;

void fire( int x, int y )
{
    Bullet b;
    b.x = x;
    b.y = y;
    b.dy = \-4;
    bullets.push_back( b );
}

void update()
{
    for( int i = 0; i < bullets.size(); )
    {
        bullets[ i ].y += bullets[ i ].dy;
        if( bullets[ i ].y < 0 )
          bullets.erase( bullets.begin() + i );
        else
          i++;
    }
}

void main()
{
    bullets.reserve( 32 );
    fire( 100, 300 );
    update();
}
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR v32c++\-array (3v32),
.BR v32c++\-string (3v32),
.BR misc.h (3v32),
.BR v32c++ (1)
.PP
.I docs/GENERICS.md
in the v32c++ source distribution.
.SH AUTHOR
Matthew Haas
